@@ -384,6 +384,22 @@ var primitiveValues = qs.parse('a=15&b=true&c=null');
 assert.deepEqual(primitiveValues, { a: '15', b: 'true', c: 'null' });
 ```
 
+To convert numeric values to numbers, set the `parseNumbers` option to `true` (it defaults to `false`).
+A decoded value `v` becomes `Number(v)` only when `v` is already in canonical decimal form, meaning `String(Number(v)) === v` and the number is finite.
+So `'1'`, `'-2'`, `'1.5'` and `'0'` are converted, while `'01'`, `'0x10'`, `'1.50'`, `'-0'`, `'.5'`, `''`, `'NaN'` and `'Infinity'` stay strings.
+The round-trip is the whole rule. `'9007199254740993'` stays a string because `Number` rounds it to `9007199254740992`, but `'123456789012345680000'` converts because it round-trips exactly.
+Exponent forms follow the same rule: `'1e3'` stays a string (JavaScript prints that number as `'1000'`), while `'1e+21'` and `'1e-7'` convert, because that is exactly how JavaScript prints those numbers.
+Only values are converted, never keys. This also applies to array elements, nested values, and values split with the `comma` option.
+Values that are not strings, such as the `null` from `strictNullHandling` or anything a custom `decoder` returns, are left as they are. Values of object input to `qs.parse` are not converted.
+
+```javascript
+var numbers = qs.parse('a=15&b=-2.5&c=007&d=1e3&e[]=1&e[]=x&1=2', { parseNumbers: true });
+assert.deepEqual(numbers, { a: 15, b: -2.5, c: '007', d: '1e3', e: [1, 'x'], 1: 2 });
+
+var commaNumbers = qs.parse('a=1,2,x', { comma: true, parseNumbers: true });
+assert.deepEqual(commaNumbers, { a: [1, 2, 'x'] });
+```
+
 If you wish to auto-convert values which look like numbers, booleans, and other values into their primitive counterparts, you can use the [query-types Express JS middleware](https://github.com/xpepermint/query-types) which will auto-convert all request query parameters.
 
